.TH CSTR 3 "November 26, 2026" "cstr 1.0" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
cstr - dynamically allocated C-style string class

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <cstr.h>

.BI "cstr(" "const char *buf" ");"
.B "cstr();"
.BI "cstr(" "const cstr &other" ");"
.BI "cstr(" "cstr &&other" ");"
.BI "~cstr();"

.BI "operator " "const char *" "() const;"
.BI "unsigned int capacity() const;"
.BI "char &operator[](" "unsigned int index" ");"

.BI "cstr &operator=(" "const cstr &other" ");"
.BI "cstr &operator=(" "cstr &&other" ");"

.BI "cstr &operator+=(" "const char *buf" ");"
.BI "cstr operator+(" "const char *buf" ") const;"

.BI "bool operator==(" "const char *buf" ") const;"
.BI "bool operator!=(" "const char *buf" ") const;"

.BI "cstr operator+(" "const char *lhs" ", const cstr &rhs" ");"
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B cstr
class provides dynamically allocated string storage
and basic string manipulation operations.

The class manages its own character buffer and provides
an interface compatible with null-terminated C-style
strings through an implicit conversion operator.

The internal character buffer is dynamically allocated
and managed by the class.

Objects maintain their own string storage, allowing
independent copies of string data.

.SH CONSTRUCTORS
.TP
.B cstr()
Constructs an empty string object.

.TP
.BI "cstr(" "const char *buf" ")"
Constructs a string object using the supplied
null-terminated character sequence.

The constructor determines the length of the input
and allocates sufficient storage for the character
sequence and its terminating null character.

.TP
.BI "cstr(" "const cstr &other" ")"
Copy constructor.

Constructs a new object by copying the contents
of another
.B cstr
object.

The resulting object maintains independent storage
from the source object.

.TP
.BI "cstr(" "cstr &&other" ")"
Move constructor.

Transfers the dynamically allocated character buffer
from the source object to the newly constructed object.

The source object is left in an empty, valid state.

.SH DESTRUCTOR
.TP
.B ~cstr()
Destroys the object and releases its dynamically
allocated character buffer.

.SH MEMBER FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B "unsigned int capacity() const"
Returns the current string length as reported by
the object's internal size value.

.TP
.BI "char &operator[](" "unsigned int index" ")"
Provides access to the character at the specified
index.

The index is zero-based.

No bounds-checking behavior is guaranteed.

.TP
.B "operator const char*() const"
Converts the object into a pointer to its
null-terminated character buffer.

This conversion allows a
.B cstr
object to be passed to functions expecting
a
.B const char *
argument.

.SH ASSIGNMENT OPERATORS
.TP
.BI "cstr &operator=(" "const cstr &other" ")"
Copy assignment operator.

Copies the contents of another object into
the current object while maintaining independent
character storage.

.TP
.BI "cstr &operator=(" "cstr &&other" ")"
Move assignment operator.

Transfers ownership of the source object's
character buffer to the destination object.

The source object is left in an empty, valid state.

.SH STRING OPERATORS
.TP
.BI "cstr &operator+=(" "const char *buf" ")"
Appends the supplied null-terminated character
sequence to the current string.

The existing string contents are preserved.

.TP
.BI "cstr operator+(" "const char *buf" ") const"
Creates and returns a new string containing
the current object's contents followed by
the supplied character sequence.

The original object remains unmodified.

.TP
.BI "bool operator==(" "const char *buf" ") const"
Compares the current string with the supplied
null-terminated character sequence.

.TP
.BI "bool operator!=(" "const char *buf" ") const"
Checks whether the current string differs
from the supplied character sequence.

.SH NON-MEMBER OPERATORS
.TP
.BI "cstr operator+(" "const char *lhs" ", const cstr &rhs" ")"
Concatenates a C-style string on the left-hand
side with a
.B cstr
object on the right-hand side.

Returns a new
.B cstr
object containing both sequences.

.SH MEMORY MANAGEMENT
The
.B cstr
class manages dynamically allocated memory.

Each independently constructed or copied object
maintains its own character buffer.

Move operations transfer ownership of the allocated
buffer instead of copying the entire character sequence.

The destructor releases the memory owned by the object.

.SH RETURN VALUE
Member functions returning a reference to
.B cstr
return the current object.

The concatenation operator
.B operator+
returns a new
.B cstr
object.

The comparison operators return a boolean value.

The
.B capacity()
function returns an unsigned integer representing
the current string length.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates basic construction,
concatenation, and output.

.nf
#include <cstr.h>

int main()
{
cstr first("Hello");
cstr second(" World");

cstr result = first + second;

wrout(result);
newL;

return 0;

}
.fi

The following example demonstrates appending.

.nf
#include <cstr.h>

int main()
{
cstr text("Hello");

text += " World";

wrout(text);
newL;

return 0;

}
.fi

.SH COMPATIBILITY
The
.B cstr
class is implemented in C++ and requires
a C++ compiler.

The implicit conversion to
.B const char *
provides interoperability with interfaces
that accept null-terminated character strings.

The class itself is not a C-compatible interface.

.SH LIMITATIONS
The
.B cstr
class does not provide a guaranteed bounds-checked
indexing operation.

The lifetime of a character pointer obtained
through the conversion operator is tied to
the lifetime and modification of its owning object.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR strlen (3),
.BR malloc (3),
.BR free (3),
.BR operator (7)

